#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133410 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a3fd63f-9991-5781-b4f8-9c4b0abc65ec" :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Flux Across a Closed Surface as a Triple Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "03c75b64-65f4-5e12-b7b1-fa8b61893eb7" :question/math-academy-id "e-12447" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ and the surfaces $z=y$ and $z=1+x^2+y^2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F} = (x+z )\\, \\mathbf{i} + yz \\, \\mathbf{j} + x^2y \\, \\mathbf{k},$ express the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ as a triple integral, and then as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n= \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(yz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}y) \\\\  & =1+z+0 \\\\  & =z+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iiint\\limits_R (z+1) \\: \\textrm{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type I solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\:-1 \\leq x \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\: y \\leq z \\leq 1+x^2+y^2 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nExpressing our triple integral as a repeated integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}z+1\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,z+1\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133411 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c239481-1424-575a-b4f7-b6f5409dd240" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across the Surface of a Known Solid"}] :question/id #uuid "9bb760a6-9060-532a-a59d-3cedf335daac" :question/math-academy-id "e-12448" :question/problem "Consider the positively oriented surface $S$ given by $x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 4$ and the vector field $\\mathbf{F} = 2x \\, \\mathbf{i} + y \\, \\mathbf{j} + 4 \\, \\mathbf{k}.$ Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n= \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4) \\\\  & =2+1+0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid sphere of radius $2$ enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}3\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =3{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{3} \\\\  & =32\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133412 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7866416-74af-5891-b8c9-f6c1a3a751ac" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Closed Surface"}] :question/id #uuid "dfba5156-6b5f-5622-9406-7598ae2630b6" :question/math-academy-id "e-12449" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=1,$ $y=0,$ $y=2,$ $z=1,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $\\mathbf{F} =e^z \\, \\mathbf{i} + x^2y^2 \\, \\mathbf{j} + 4x^2yz \\, \\mathbf{k},$ find the flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n= \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{z})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4{x}^{2}yz) \\\\  & =0+2{x}^{2}y+4{x}^{2}y \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}2y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}]_{x=0}^{x=1}\\cdot [{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=2}\\cdot (2-1) \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (4-0)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134058 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a3fd63f-9991-5781-b4f8-9c4b0abc65ec" :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Flux Across a Closed Surface as a Triple Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2eb57e2-583e-5ae5-b1e6-4b195a14601f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-2z}^{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\,{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371371} {:answer/id #uuid "ea5bfdd6-0806-5a67-8e3e-b18a2af64a34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}^{y-2z}\\,2y+1\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371372} {:answer/id #uuid "2c222030-976a-5a50-b59b-c7eaabdf5540" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-2}^{4}\\,z+3y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371373} {:answer/id #uuid "37e9ace8-60d1-5653-9f3e-743821adccfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-2z}^{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\,2y+1\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371374} {:answer/id #uuid "8bb0cea1-145d-5c9e-a6d6-b83c4f4b97e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}^{y-2z}\\,{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371375}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2eb57e2-583e-5ae5-b1e6-4b195a14601f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-2z}^{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\,{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371371} :answer-field/id #uuid "5602e98c-f1af-58b7-98ca-82b8eabdd4f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371370}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "152cbc4e-4f16-538e-9423-127439b0fd3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-162408" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $y=-1,$ $y=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=1,$ and the surfaces $x=y-2z$ and $x=4-{z}^{2}-{y}^{2}.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=x{y}^{2}\\,i+xz\\,j+y\\,k,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(y) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}+0+0 \\\\  & ={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type II solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,y-2z\\le x\\le 4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-2z}^{4-{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\,{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134059 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c239481-1424-575a-b4f7-b6f5409dd240" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across the Surface of a Known Solid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "859b117f-0729-59ab-b4e4-a881ff62e113" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2000π}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130918} {:answer/id #uuid "85fb4805-6d0d-57ca-ae3b-08829d611f62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{400π}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130919} {:answer/id #uuid "e530475b-d73d-5e5a-abbb-8d686f636627" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25π^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130920} {:answer/id #uuid "fe99305d-b04d-5d72-be9c-15d4c1386dc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16π}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130921} {:answer/id #uuid "05964317-b8c8-5f35-8934-eb5d16570879" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{800π}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130922}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "859b117f-0729-59ab-b4e4-a881ff62e113" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2000π}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130918} :answer-field/id #uuid "784ecdef-4ec4-5019-9c9d-a1a1458e0d5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130917}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "088370e5-5be7-5c78-9dd1-180055943e46" :question/math-academy-id "q-162410" :question/problem "Consider the positively (outward) oriented surface $S$ given by ${x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}+(z+1)^{2}=25$ and the vector field $F=x\\,i+xz\\,j+3z\\,k.$ Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3z) \\\\  & =1+0+3 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid sphere of radius $5$ enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}4\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =4{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\cdot {5}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\,000\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371376 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c239481-1424-575a-b4f7-b6f5409dd240" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across the Surface of a Known Solid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e853cda6-c206-5e40-bcb4-802df8153563" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371378} {:answer/id #uuid "dd0ee171-3960-5d62-b7dc-3048dcb45d12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371379} {:answer/id #uuid "161693cd-c882-54e3-92be-61f5c5fc7afb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371380} {:answer/id #uuid "42b46116-de75-5acd-9690-4e12c330929d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371381} {:answer/id #uuid "0d7adaa7-91c0-5947-849b-6e32a6a5d351" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371382}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42b46116-de75-5acd-9690-4e12c330929d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371381} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec78130e-b332-5bf6-9e83-6fc29a18b6ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371377}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cccfe8f0-fae6-5eb5-97b7-607f248d5344" :question/math-academy-id "q-162569" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/assets/581ac462bfcfead22a6c5ad4eb247f05adb1a677e92cac51954b0746cd083ab7.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨x-y,\\,y+z,\\,2x-3z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $3$ whose base is a circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Given that $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y+z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2x-3z) \\\\  & =1+1+(-3) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid cylinder enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}-1\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =-{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =-\\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =-\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-12\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371383 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c239481-1424-575a-b4f7-b6f5409dd240" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across the Surface of a Known Solid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17c8f4c9-337e-549f-9444-4204dc6085de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371385} {:answer/id #uuid "68efd64f-4583-553c-ad4a-b8f9a8843333" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371386} {:answer/id #uuid "b861f96d-c51c-571c-82e0-8bac5a5eda7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371387} {:answer/id #uuid "91817b8b-b2af-58d6-99cd-23dead19d030" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371388} {:answer/id #uuid "c4c9d965-5bd1-506c-b3b4-1a1f96cd8f8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371389}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91817b8b-b2af-58d6-99cd-23dead19d030" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371388} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd8f3189-2922-5ad5-80f7-e76b43bad640" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371384}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7c2bde26-4c23-54bd-9680-448652feb44d" :question/math-academy-id "q-162572" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14071461/assets/aac27058402f89bef73700129a7812b3cf4f971c11975fc1711a46b8d10e61fe.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨3x+y{e}^{z},\\,xy+2,\\,1-xz⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Given that $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x+y{e}^{z})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy+2)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(1-xz) \\\\  & =3+x+(-x) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the rectangular solid enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}3\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =3{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1\\cdot 2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371390 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c239481-1424-575a-b4f7-b6f5409dd240" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across the Surface of a Known Solid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "679bb337-2eae-5325-b65f-15321136ae81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{128\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371392} {:answer/id #uuid "10066137-c52f-5eb8-ad46-922eaeb7c60f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{256\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371393} {:answer/id #uuid "3d11e33c-5c19-5afd-be27-6c3f6a827ace" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371394} {:answer/id #uuid "cacd617f-f18f-5e8a-8835-616879b95e55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371395} {:answer/id #uuid "eb5cc3ad-a5b9-5e7b-bf22-d76b02cab9e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "78\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cacd617f-f18f-5e8a-8835-616879b95e55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371395} :answer-field/id #uuid "d50f0f5d-1a3a-5af5-bb0c-c2579e1e5951" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371391}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d8dbe31e-6bdd-543e-b097-44ed741785c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-162578" :question/problem "Consider the positively (outward) oriented surface $S$ given by ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+(z-4)^{2}=4$ and the vector field $F=z\\,i+6y\\,j+(x+3z)\\,k.$ Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(6y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x+3z) \\\\  & =0+6+3 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid sphere of radius $2$ enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}9\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =9{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{3} \\\\  & =96\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371397 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c239481-1424-575a-b4f7-b6f5409dd240" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across the Surface of a Known Solid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e638f438-e1e3-56c9-bd6b-174ba4f5d383" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371399} {:answer/id #uuid "0d52b91e-ccf6-58f2-b6b6-81c579b27d80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371400} {:answer/id #uuid "576b65e8-29bb-5e90-9496-d6481c6e4fac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371401} {:answer/id #uuid "4d5b5e91-ad46-570d-884d-51d779981a44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371402} {:answer/id #uuid "96b6bc14-4c42-50a6-bc5d-37113c5dc0f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371403}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "576b65e8-29bb-5e90-9496-d6481c6e4fac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371401} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf337090-c8b2-5aea-bf56-8bd215441650" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371398}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "54b0961b-2c4a-5e03-819c-f8b7975f6411" :question/math-academy-id "q-162580" :question/problem "Consider the positively (outward) oriented surface $S$ given by $(x+2)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ and the vector field $F=({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\,i+4y\\,j+2xz\\,k.$ Use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2xz) \\\\  & =-2x+4+2x \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid sphere of radius $1$ enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}4\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =4{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\cdot {1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371404 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a3fd63f-9991-5781-b4f8-9c4b0abc65ec" :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Flux Across a Closed Surface as a Triple Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4780b6ad-585b-5e4a-9f10-2ee683ef5d8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+y+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371406} {:answer/id #uuid "a12d3323-b03b-516d-94a9-8b30a8375979" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371407} {:answer/id #uuid "d173047a-4989-581f-a8b1-6d0129392a1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+2y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371408} {:answer/id #uuid "152ca7d0-c3e9-5c3a-85d3-d385c61ca427" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371409} {:answer/id #uuid "33ed3400-2f68-5a63-a62a-fa77bf50169c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+y+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371410}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33ed3400-2f68-5a63-a62a-fa77bf50169c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}+y+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371410} :answer-field/id #uuid "03d65ba8-1bf3-51f3-a801-1b074613f084" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371405}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e0d14173-8029-50a6-8f00-d7853c057071" :question/math-academy-id "q-162581" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=0,$ $y=1,$ $y=2,$ $z=0,$ $z=1.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=xy\\,i+2y\\,j+{x}^{2}zk,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}z) \\\\  & =y+2+{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+y+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}({x}^{2}+y+2)\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,{x}^{2}+y+2\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371411 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a3fd63f-9991-5781-b4f8-9c4b0abc65ec" :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Flux Across a Closed Surface as a Triple Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eed5b62a-cd47-5498-93ea-5748a0cdf7bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+3z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371413} {:answer/id #uuid "ab811755-3b35-502e-bee8-a0c3d59acbfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-z+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371414} {:answer/id #uuid "1b9753ae-72b5-58ce-8026-675977769eed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x-z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371415} {:answer/id #uuid "cb234d0c-4336-51b2-b53e-3886cee8f26a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x+4z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371416} {:answer/id #uuid "73dcd527-2255-580c-a940-74ec402491b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-y-2z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371417}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b9753ae-72b5-58ce-8026-675977769eed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x-z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371415} :answer-field/id #uuid "614dbe19-4218-5e05-8664-99c4d9231395" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371412}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "20faa8ac-7475-55a0-94fb-35eb1571259e" :question/math-academy-id "q-162583" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=4,$ $y=-1,$ $y=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=({x}^{2}+y)\\,i-yz\\,j+4xz\\,k,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing part of the expression?" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-yz)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4xz) \\\\  & =2x-z+4x \\\\  & =6x-z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}(6x-z)\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,6x-z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371418 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a3fd63f-9991-5781-b4f8-9c4b0abc65ec" :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Flux Across a Closed Surface as a Triple Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a53e62d2-c3c1-5bf3-b7ed-f50ed4de2b07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,y+z-2\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371420} {:answer/id #uuid "12983a0d-93e7-558b-ba73-b4d108c6cba0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,{x}^{2}-1\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371421} {:answer/id #uuid "95f2bbf7-051c-57da-9c30-16a10e22bb41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,2xz\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371422} {:answer/id #uuid "33270575-6686-533e-a787-ee503a855d8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2y}^{2{x}^{2}+1}\\,{x}^{2}-1\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371423} {:answer/id #uuid "1455dc99-7527-5fac-8ea9-65123797883f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2y}^{2{x}^{2}+1}\\,y+z-2\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371424}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12983a0d-93e7-558b-ba73-b4d108c6cba0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,{x}^{2}-1\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371421} :answer-field/id #uuid "f364bf57-698c-59e1-9fee-d884c9fd3252" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371419}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "07b2df57-316b-561c-bce7-7ecfebe212fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-162588" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=2,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ and the surfaces $z=y$ and $z={x}^{2}+y.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=(x+y)\\,i-2y\\,j+{x}^{2}z\\,k,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}z) \\\\  & =1+(-2)+{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}({x}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type I solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,y\\le z\\le {x}^{2}+y}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{x}^{2}-1\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,{x}^{2}-1\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371425 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a3fd63f-9991-5781-b4f8-9c4b0abc65ec" :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Flux Across a Closed Surface as a Triple Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04ca41fa-fa6e-5e8e-89da-66fc415279f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2}^{{z}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,3{y}^{2}+xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371427} {:answer/id #uuid "9eff9ad9-d3a6-5a6f-9f24-bbd6e67b2971" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,2xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371428} {:answer/id #uuid "82519da3-22f7-5b2a-b72f-b730c0825fd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}^{2}\\,2xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371429} {:answer/id #uuid "813ac4a4-e0e0-552e-822d-24a6ad256672" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}^{2}\\,3{y}^{2}+xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371430} {:answer/id #uuid "72dc0460-a429-54c0-9129-56040ffb9907" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,x+y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82519da3-22f7-5b2a-b72f-b730c0825fd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}^{2}\\,2xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371429} :answer-field/id #uuid "88d3b66b-ebc2-5e30-af7c-cde4cb13f65f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371426}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "838b34a2-ad5a-5f20-9f09-00b68f844e2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-162589" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the finite solid region enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=1,$ and the surfaces $y={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and $y=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=({y}^{3}-z)\\,i+z\\,j+2xyz\\,k,$ the flux of $F$ across $S$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{3}-z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2xyz) \\\\  & =0+0+2xy \\\\  & =2xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}2xy\\,\\text{d}V,\n$$\n\nwhere $R$ is a type III solid region that can be written as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le y\\le 2}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}2xy\\,\\text{d}V & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}}^{2}\\,2xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371432 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7866416-74af-5891-b8c9-f6c1a3a751ac" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Closed Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a192554d-263f-515b-bd37-a122e23c8957" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371434} {:answer/id #uuid "0104f547-7285-5a52-b44d-8271a0798e61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371435} {:answer/id #uuid "0cb277df-23d2-51e0-a224-947f2c149adb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371436} {:answer/id #uuid "ebc479bd-f327-5b0f-9788-1b8eda54bdb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371437} {:answer/id #uuid "1a167791-55f1-5ac8-aebc-363172a73126" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371438}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a167791-55f1-5ac8-aebc-363172a73126" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371438} :answer-field/id #uuid "77379f98-513e-50e3-a943-63caa4b9dafa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "25c7ce92-3c53-5961-9526-9951885113bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-162594" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=2,$ $y=0,$ $y=4,$ $z=1,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F={x}^{2}y\\,i+2z\\,j+xyz\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xyz) \\\\  & =2xy+0+xy \\\\  & =3xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}3xy\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{2}3xy\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{4}y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}3\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=4}\\cdot [3z]_{z=1}^{z=2} \\\\  & =(2-0)\\cdot (8-0)\\cdot (6-3) \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371439 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7866416-74af-5891-b8c9-f6c1a3a751ac" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Closed Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f03ea61e-fb65-59bd-9087-891b44a6e5ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371441} {:answer/id #uuid "8f337480-aa00-5ae0-8039-bda944fe767f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371442} {:answer/id #uuid "84a977cf-0aae-51e3-a516-b036d315e448" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371443} {:answer/id #uuid "a179c58f-587f-5ba3-8b70-466cad576816" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371444} {:answer/id #uuid "ec38f0f1-d0d7-5aed-9262-9fea22240afc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371445}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec38f0f1-d0d7-5aed-9262-9fea22240afc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371445} :answer-field/id #uuid "f256505b-c2ca-531e-96ca-e345eed18b67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b7f302e2-c66a-5f62-9bda-122fb92df5ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-162596" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=1,$ $x=e,$ $y=0,$ $y=2,$ $z=0,$ $z=2.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,i+{z}^{2}\\,j+(x-{y}^{3})\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x-{y}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x}+0+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]_{x=1}^{x=e}\\cdot {[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\cdot [z]_{z=0}^{z=2} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\cdot (\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2})\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371446 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7866416-74af-5891-b8c9-f6c1a3a751ac" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Closed Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4ce8af8-1cab-53b7-b4be-fbaab6ecc90b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371448} {:answer/id #uuid "8908ab80-4a54-5c16-bebf-26d72f20ef7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371449} {:answer/id #uuid "59fb4934-67cf-5be5-85c4-8b99d21b2f5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371450} {:answer/id #uuid "61552f87-bae4-5e36-89d2-0529f45676bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371451} {:answer/id #uuid "b4c2b43a-6198-54f7-8556-699ba097fda6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371452}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61552f87-bae4-5e36-89d2-0529f45676bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371451} :answer-field/id #uuid "42f89423-06ad-5708-9940-540ddda32b60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371447}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "90fe91b8-bc99-59fe-8878-7778d578ba2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-162599" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=1,$ $y=1,$ $y=2,$ $z=1,$ $z=3.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F={x}^{2}\\,i+2y\\,j+(x+{y}^{2})\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2x+2+0 \\\\  & =2x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}(2x+2)\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}(2x+2)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+2)\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{3}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}+2x]_{x=0}^{x=1}\\cdot (2-1)\\cdot (3-1) \\\\  & =({1}^{2}+2(1)-0)\\cdot 1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371453 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7866416-74af-5891-b8c9-f6c1a3a751ac" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Closed Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6336215b-4e92-5986-a1f2-f8308ce6b70c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371455} {:answer/id #uuid "8082c90c-262a-515c-bb00-959e0bfb6950" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371456} {:answer/id #uuid "8083d565-6616-589b-a364-3e848a83ad8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371457} {:answer/id #uuid "91782d49-bdac-5103-9436-b5a3b8a1865c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371458} {:answer/id #uuid "a749f4a7-9a8f-5425-8664-3be06b91d636" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371459}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91782d49-bdac-5103-9436-b5a3b8a1865c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371458} :answer-field/id #uuid "c15596a5-4ef4-51ee-bf37-5bd8df47d933" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371454}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c34b8d27-a2d0-5c0a-b0c5-368721d0dede" :question/math-academy-id "q-162600" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=1,$ $y=1,$ $y=2,$ $z=-1,$ $z=1.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=2y\\,i+x{y}^{2}\\,j+2xyz\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2xyz) \\\\  & =0+2xy+2xy \\\\  & =4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}4xy\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}4xy\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}2y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=1}\\cdot [{y}^{2}]_{y=1}^{y=2}\\cdot (1-(-1)) \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (4-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134062 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7866416-74af-5891-b8c9-f6c1a3a751ac" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux Across a Closed Surface"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88cff0d8-84ea-59c3-a7fc-45b335de10be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130936} {:answer/id #uuid "ce20e89c-5803-55e9-9e73-f2a7555339d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130937} {:answer/id #uuid "dbfdb111-57f9-5e23-bab0-4cabd5862285" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130938} {:answer/id #uuid "0f3dd18e-28ce-5678-a0f4-a73e9028d538" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130939} {:answer/id #uuid "48fdc01a-45bd-51a8-8700-c1bba2e7d3f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130940}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce20e89c-5803-55e9-9e73-f2a7555339d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130937} :answer-field/id #uuid "932902f6-0be4-5e94-89c7-a5a73841a49a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186130935}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b54a2292-f496-593a-8f3d-c0762d77f082" :question/math-academy-id "q-162606" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0,$ $x=2,$ $y=0,$ $y=1,$ $z=0,$ $z=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,j+(x+y)\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x+y) \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z+0+0 \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=2}\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot [\\operatorname{sin}⁡z]_{z=0}^{z=\\pi /2} \\\\  & =({2}^{2}-{0}^{2})\\cdot 1\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
